<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Latest news from the Middle East, daily international news reports - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage and analyses of political and social issues across the Middle East, including Egypt, the Gulf states, Iraq, Israel, Jordan, Lebanon, Saudi Arabia, Syria, Yemen and beyond.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/rss</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Are fresh US-Israeli strikes on Iran in the pipeline?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5386c94e6403f45bf369b2f862f5d868151431ef3783491ebceeca88b75db2b9</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-22T11:32:22Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261022-are-fresh-us-israeli-strikes-on-iran-in-the-pipeline" /><summary type="html">PRESS REVIEW – Thursday, October 22: The New York Times's sources report plans for fresh strikes on Iran after the US midterm elections. In other news, the French Senate publishes a 30-step proposal for how to "take back control", just days after protests gave way to riots. Also, how software engineers found and patched up bugs in the language that makes up 70 percent of the internet. Finally, the story of a man for whom what happened in Vegas did not stay there.</summary><category term="Press Review" /></entry><entry><title>French President Macron lands in Saudi Arabia on surprise 'solidarity' visit</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4e4a49f6363549f7d0752194f64371121cf26d6674e88255621e0f269c64591f</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-21T15:54:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/french-president-macron-lands-in-saudi-arabia-on-surprise-solidarity-visit" /><summary type="html">⁠French President Emmanuel ​Macron landed in Riyadh on Wednseday, ‌adding an ⁠unscheduled stop to his ‌ongoing tour of the Gulf ⁠region. The president's office said the visit aimed to demonstrate “France's solidarity” ​with Saudi Arabia in ‌the face of attacks launched by the ‌Houthis against its territory. Details by FRANCE 24 international affairs editor Douglas Herbert.</summary></entry><entry><title>Egypt mulls joining Saudi Arabia in war against Houthis</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5a0ff0c1c4cdc1dd783a38ee8075adf0b184e1660fe7806e9342806274e12044</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-21T11:02:21Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/egypt-mulls-joining-saudi-arabia-in-war-against-houthis--71c10c680ac4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/egypt-mulls-joining-saudi-arabia-in-war-against-houthis" /><summary type="html">Egypt may be about to join the war against Houthis. Saudi Arabia has come under sustained attack from the Iran-backed group in recent weeks after a collapse of a truce in Yemen in September. Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman visited Egypt mid-September to request potential military support from the country – a demand put before parliament by the Egyptian president on Saturday. Story by Camille Corcoran.</summary></entry><entry><title>REPLAY: French president addresses fuel supplies during Gulf tour</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a44a521fa70025bca03c0fca58147ce24ada25e33cf47c6b89bda4a67f491503</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-21T10:18:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/replay-french-president-addresses-fuel-supplies-during-gulf-tour--340b4074fa18.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/replay-french-president-addresses-fuel-supplies-during-gulf-tour" /><summary type="html">French President Emmanuel Macron said on Tuesday he had secured the delivery of an additional 200,000 tonnes of diesel per month from the United Arab Emirates and Bahrain. He is currently in Doha as part of a tour of the Gulf. "We are reducing our dependency" he said whilst speaking to the press on October 21.</summary></entry><entry><title>Egyptian involvement in Yemen 'may actually weaken Cairo's regional role', expert says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:57a9ce019123758c287fb0f9abd1a7e2b8e30e25d5bdc60ac31bbf0f233cd231</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T21:18:48Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/a-propos/20261020-egypt-involvement-in-yemen-may-actually-weaken-egypt-s-regional-role-expert-says--50c279eebc7a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/a-propos/20261020-egypt-involvement-in-yemen-may-actually-weaken-egypt-s-regional-role-expert-says" /><summary type="html">Egypt's parliament on Monday delayed a debate on President Abdel Fattah al-Sisi's request to authorise military support for Saudi Arabia until next week, state media reported, after an emergency session ended without a decision a day earlier. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Dr May Darwich, Associate Professor in International Relations of the Middle East at the University of Birmingham. She explains that "this kind of military operation does not really have any objectives or exit strategy so at the moment this might actually weaken Egypt's regional role".</summary><category term="A propos" /></entry><entry><title>Egyptian involvement in Yemen 'may actually weaken Cairo's regional role', expert says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6bb3a5b1231715475e2298bddc0f038bb92fb6a0b028d1d3dc87f24982df8b53</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T20:57:29Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/egypt-involvement-in-yemen-may-actually-weaken-egypt-s-regional-role-expert-says--f5175f05879c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/egypt-involvement-in-yemen-may-actually-weaken-egypt-s-regional-role-expert-says" /><summary type="html">Egypt's parliament on Monday delayed a debate on President Abdel Fattah al-Sisi's request to authorise military support for Saudi Arabia until next week, state media reported, after an emergency session ended without a decision a day earlier. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Dr May Darwich, Associate Professor in International Relations of the Middle East at the University of Birmingham. She explains that "this kind of military operation does not really have any objectives or exit strategy so at the moment this might actually weaken Egypt's regional role".</summary></entry><entry><title>'Netanyahu is fighting for his political career', expert says a week ahead of crucial vote</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a36c9b7e33120571b5fa33946190657c03edaeb5110335a244123c8f104148f5</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T20:03:50Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/netanyahu-is-fighting-for-his-political-career-expert-says-a-week-ahead-of-crucial-vote--ab66e2789282.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/netanyahu-is-fighting-for-his-political-career-expert-says-a-week-ahead-of-crucial-vote" /><summary type="html">Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu basked in Donald Trump's endorsement on Monday ahead of close-fought elections, airing a phone call in which the US president predicted he would win and suggested they may again attack Iran together. FRANCE 24's Noga Tarnopolsky reports from Jerusalem. FRANCE 24's Mark Owen speaks with Simon Mabon, Professor of International Politics and Middle East Studies at Lancaster University.</summary></entry><entry><title>France's decades-long ties with Gulf states built on 'economic development, oil, defence agreements'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d2b50d25a32850adc003b221b86dabfe95afc76751e2c65c89ad68f76ccced1f</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T15:52:47Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261020-france-s-decades-long-ties-with-gulf-states-built-on-economic-development-oil-defense-agree--429a4eb3fad2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261020-france-s-decades-long-ties-with-gulf-states-built-on-economic-development-oil-defense-agreements-1" /><summary type="html">Genie Godula is pleased to welcome François Gouyette, former French ambassador to the UAE and Saudi Arabia. Against a backdrop of violent unrest at home and escalating conflict in the Middle East, French President Emmanuel Macron's Gulf diplomacy underscores the increasingly blurred boundaries between domestic economic security and international strategic commitments. Ambassador Gouyette argues that the visit serves a dual purpose: alleviating France's energy pressures while reaffirming its longstanding defense partnerships with Gulf monarchies. Insisting that "France has to play its role as a key partner," Gouyette portrays French military assistance as an expression of strategic solidarity rather than direct participation in the war with Iran.</summary></entry><entry><title>‘I don't recognise the country I grew up in anymore’: Some Israelis tempted by exile</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2449e82c093399d00dcbad8e9f97b95d3d96d9af37859372b079dfb01f4bb4f1</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T13:07:27Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261020-i-don-t-recognise-the-country-i-grew-up-in-anymore-some-israelis-are-tempted-by-exile--7f8340f3ecd8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261020-i-don-t-recognise-the-country-i-grew-up-in-anymore-some-israelis-are-tempted-by-exile" /><summary type="html">From our special correspondent in Tel Aviv – Since 2023, Israel has experienced an unprecedented brain drain. With the October 27 legislative elections approaching, many graduates are considering leaving the country if the right-wing and far-right coalition led by Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu remains in power.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Macron secures diesel deals on Gulf visit as fuel prices stoke unrest in France</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e7217552ca2f61a00a80fa0f2a9d4d6dbc19f939e4fbaaa3cd1b83d0fc22e4a4</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T09:44:49Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261020-macron-secures-diesel-deals-on-gulf-visit-as-fuel-prices-stoke-unrest-in-france--c5d5248f7d38.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261020-macron-secures-diesel-deals-on-gulf-visit-as-fuel-prices-stoke-unrest-in-france" /><summary type="html">French President Emmanuel Macron has obtained commitments to deliver an additional 200,000 tonnes of diesel per month from the United Arab Emirates and Bahrain during a tour of the Gulf, his office said Tuesday as the French government scrambles to address a cost-of-living crisis that has fuelled unrest in the country.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>France 'has to assist' Gulf states, diplomat says as Macron makes three-day visit</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:11f9d30c8ddb4a8cd041a5bc1e4144735c5881c60423e1e0433f4949c1246643</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T12:43:41Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261019-france-has-to-assist-gulf-states-diplomat-says-as-macron-makes-three-day-visit--6a87f317e16d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261019-france-has-to-assist-gulf-states-diplomat-says-as-macron-makes-three-day-visit" /><summary type="html">French President Emmanuel Macron is making a three-day visit to the Gulf states focused on fuel supplies and strategic ties. The French leader will first visit Kuwait before heading to the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and Qatar. The Elysée Palace said Macron would seek commitments for additional deliveries of diesel and petrol that could increase supplies available to France.</summary><category term="Perspective" /></entry><entry><title>Inflation squeezes households as Lebanon's economic crisis persists</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:091335514a2b26676b2f624afc05a1e2480c5b92a230d241728f47bf09c34c03</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T12:18:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261019-inflation-squeezes-households-as-lebanon-s-economic-crisis-persists--bc698d04a646.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261019-inflation-squeezes-households-as-lebanon-s-economic-crisis-persists" /><summary type="html">Lebanon's economic crisis has dragged on since 2019. A re-igniting of Israel's war with Hezbollah derailed the weak recovery seen last year. The World Bank now forecasts that the Lebanese economy will shrink by 6.4 percent this year, with inflation expected to reach around 17.5 percent. A growing divide is visible everywhere: in households, in businesses and even in consumer habits. Our correspondents Serge Berberi and Renée Davis report.</summary><category term="Focus" /></entry><entry><title>World Food Programme to end aid for half a million in Gaza due to funding shortfall</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:03870e5db65aefe2e05b1cb1176aca0ebc20e9be73a0734834c6ceb314e3449d</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T07:33:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261019-world-food-programme-to-end-aid-for-half-a-million-in-gaza-due-to-funding-shortfall--2b4968fb4b71.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261019-world-food-programme-to-end-aid-for-half-a-million-in-gaza-due-to-funding-shortfall" /><summary type="html">The UN World Food Programme has warned that funding shortages will leave about 500,000 people in the Gaza Strip without critical food aid in a territory where two thirds of the population remains acutely food insecure. The WFP said it is reducing its monthly assistance from 1.6 million to 1.1 million people.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Trump endorses embattled Israeli PM Netanyahu ahead of October 27 election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a76d2fee4172b5216b1ba4a1cc68fadd75aa2c85ca1676606ee1ae3089a3cf85</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-18T16:25:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261018-trump-endorses-embattled-israeli-pm-netanyahu-ahead-of-october-27-election--a8f46cc8a76c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261018-trump-endorses-embattled-israeli-pm-netanyahu-ahead-of-october-27-election" /><summary type="html">US President Donald Trump on Sunday endorsed Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu ahead of Israel's October 27 elections. It was not clear what impact the endorsement would have on voters, since many Israelis have expressed disappointment with Trump over the outcome of the Iran war and the US president's ceasefire proposal.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Israeli settlers attack European delegation convoy in West Bank, beat French mayor</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:77f998e18c1fdfcb2b5a26acd0fbe2739a18275b7919217892bef4cc6154ce2a</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-17T14:58:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261017-israeli-settlers-attack-european-delegation-convoy-in-west-bank-beat-french-mayor--a06f848970c2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261017-israeli-settlers-attack-european-delegation-convoy-in-west-bank-beat-french-mayor" /><summary type="html">Israeli settlers on Saturday attacked a convoy carrying a European delegation in the occupied West Bank, beating up a 78-year-old  French mayor. While Israel's Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu – who is just days from a crucial election – described the incident as a "smear" campaign against Israeli settlers, France's top diplomat urged Israel to "urgently halt criminal violence".</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Yemen's Houthis claim responsibility for fresh missile strike on Aden airport</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:66ec482828c6ea365c93a8b08e7f308401765cea920fbcbad1270a1fc03eed23</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-17T06:00:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261017-yemen-s-houthis-claim-responsibility-for-fresh-missile-strike-on-aden-airport--9f16ebaf4ca2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261017-yemen-s-houthis-claim-responsibility-for-fresh-missile-strike-on-aden-airport" /><summary type="html">Yemen's Iran-aligned Houthi group claimed responsibility after two missiles struck Aden International Airport on Friday, saying they had targeted a weapons depot as well as a military command headquarters. The Aden Security Department said a Yemenia Airways flight bound for Cairo had been preparing for takeoff when the attack took place.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>US journalist injured during Israeli settler attack on West Bank olive harvest</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:23fa4b3c1adf0d433b49094272efdb904ff828bc55e3b4505ebd8460bd6e577e</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-16T15:01:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261016-us-journalist-injured-during-israeli-settler-attack-on-west-bank-olive-harvest--89b61e5d5406.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261016-us-journalist-injured-during-israeli-settler-attack-on-west-bank-olive-harvest" /><summary type="html">About 80 Israeli settlers set fire to olive trees and threw stones at Palestinians in a West Bank village Friday, injuring at least two people, including a US photojournalist with The Washington Post. The coordinated attack came as violence by settlers is on the rise during the olive harvest in the occupied Palestinian territory.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Saudi Arabia postpones major sporting events amid Houthi attacks</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6b4143fcc23565186dccf33dda1da6dc96cc6db90b29c5bddbbe4cd2cc95fb78</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-15T20:31:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/saudi-arabia-postpones-major-sporting-events-amid-houthi-attacks--b77da744bba3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/saudi-arabia-postpones-major-sporting-events-amid-houthi-attacks" /><summary type="html">Several major sporting events in Saudi Arabia have been cancelled or postponed as a result of repeated deadly attacks from Iran-backed Houthi rebels against the Kingdom's airports and infrastructure. FRANCE 24's Mark Owen speaks with Guy Barton, Visiting Fellow at Lancaster University and co-creator and co-host of The FootPol Podcast.</summary></entry><entry><title>Houthi attack on palace used by Yemen government in Aden kills five</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:46e9493dc0d3c10ff6aa820dbd562f053715abb2c03ad5698357e1fa23a7b361</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-15T19:57:48Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261015-houthi-attack-on-palace-used-by-yemen-government-in-aden-kills-five--c8f66163cd30.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261015-houthi-attack-on-palace-used-by-yemen-government-in-aden-kills-five" /><summary type="html">At least five people were killed and 16 wounded Thursday in a Houthi missile and drone attack on the palace located in Yemen's interim capital – the southern port city of Aden, used by the country's internationally recognised government, an official said.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Election delay 'a terrible disappointment', Palestinian activist Samer Sinijlawi says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:026288d081fa3c1a7e24c4716fa466dc8bca7c67344ac0b92845c31403d8b2c2</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-15T16:17:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t_te-_-t_te/20261015-election-delay-a-terrible-disappointment-palestinian-activist-samer-sinijlawi-says--df16dcb36eae.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20261015-election-delay-a-terrible-disappointment-palestinian-activist-samer-sinijlawi-says" /><summary type="html">In an interview with FRANCE 24, Palestinian activist Samer Sinijlawi called the postponement of the first legislative elections in 20 years to September 2027 "a terrible disappointment" for a generation that has never had a chance to vote. He also dismissed the citing of Israeli restrictions as an excuse, pointing instead to a "rejection" of change among ageing Palestinian leaders.</summary><category term="Tête à tête" /></entry><entry><title>War escalates between Houthis and Riyadh: How did we get here?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:72089f49f9ee01e76fd06ffc0d51614a3a194b6223d5739b9fb0cebce98d8247</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-15T14:16:57Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261015-war-escalates-between-houthis-and-riyadh-how-did-we-get-here--f6da2e88a028.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261015-war-escalates-between-houthis-and-riyadh-how-did-we-get-here" /><summary type="html">To discuss the ongoing conflict between the Houthi rebels and the Saudi-backed coalition, we speak to Jules Grange Gastinel, a researcher at France's CNRS research body specialising in the Houthi movement. He tells us why the conflict has escalated and what the Houthis want.</summary><category term="Perspective" /></entry><entry><title>Houthis deny targeting holy city of Medina after Saudi Arabia says it intercepted missile</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d65f918d459c6f3224dedc6fe18dcaa2c33a7fc1be42a06a5d052b586902b5c6</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-15T10:24:35Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261015-houthis-deny-targeting-holy-city-medina-after-saudi-arabia-says-intercepted-missile--83bfe6cb0a9b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261015-houthis-deny-targeting-holy-city-medina-after-saudi-arabia-says-intercepted-missile" /><summary type="html">Yemen's Houthis denied carrying out an attack on Saudi Arabia on Thursday, shortly after Riyadh said it had intercepted a missile targeting the region around the holy city of Medina. Riyadh also accused Tehran of stoking tensions between the Houthis and government forces in the latest escalation.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>‘Growing ambitions’: Dr. Kendall on Houthis’ quest to be recognised as Yemen’s ‘legitimate' leaders</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ce9329e5892d9a2443d7d1c64ec975815d96980be7a66a6fdb7c6f6bfa52fa71</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-14T17:18:04Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/growing-ambitions-dr-kendall-on-houthis-quest-to-be-recognised-as-yemen-s-legitimate-leaders--1fcca6275846.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/growing-ambitions-dr-kendall-on-houthis-quest-to-be-recognised-as-yemen-s-legitimate-leaders" /><summary type="html">What is the strategic logic behind the Houthis’ increasingly high-risk confrontation with Saudi Arabia? “Their experience in the past is that threats pay,” explains Dr. Elizabeth Kendall, Middle East specialist and President of Girton College, University of Cambridge. Dr. Kendall argues that the movement’s increasingly audacious military operations are not merely expressions of ideological hostility or Iranian influence, but calculated instruments of political coercion aimed at securing legitimacy, expanding territorial control and strengthening its negotiating leverage.</summary></entry><entry><title>Houthis threaten escalation after strike on Saudi oil giant Aramco</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:af2b1ba454d14981922794238ea870a5a23f93d6a2747a16162ec90d6d7e4c57</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-14T16:21:53Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261014-yemen-houthis-vow-continue-saudi-attacks-oil-giant-aramco-headquarters-hit--35d454874d36.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261014-yemen-houthis-vow-continue-saudi-attacks-oil-giant-aramco-headquarters-hit" /><summary type="html">Yemen's Houthi rebels said Wednesday they had targeted facilities belonging to Saudi oil giant Aramco after officials in Riyadh reported a strike on a building in an area housing the energy giant's headquarters. The Iran-back militia also claimed missile strikes on the capital Riyadh and vowed to continue attacking Saudi airports and oil facilities.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Blasts rock Riyadh as Houthis vow to continue striking Saudi airports, oil facilities</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3e67c0ffe1d63f04123ecef5e032bf15ad85fb1570482e575e8516a6da55acfe</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-14T03:39:11Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261014-live-saudi-arabia-reports-aramco-building-hit-and-four-wounded-in-houthi-missile-attacks--804ca1ce2b22.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261014-live-saudi-arabia-reports-aramco-building-hit-and-four-wounded-in-houthi-missile-attacks" /><summary type="html">Yemen's Iran-backed Houthis said on Wednesday they had targeted facilities belonging to Saudi oil giant Aramco as well as Riyadh airport after loud blasts rattled the Saudi capital. The Saudi-led coalition fighting the Houthis earlier said it had intercepted a missile fired towards Riyadh. Read about the day’s events as they unfolded on our liveblog.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Oil facility near Yemen capital Sanaa hit by Saudi Arabia, Houthis say</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f9dc5d531da35a9afadd999d97f454beeed00bcd83cb09ee34ddf5e58bbee4bb</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-13T14:53:39Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261013-oil-facility-near-yemen-capital-sanaa-hit-by-saudi-arabia-houthis-say--987287f5942b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261013-oil-facility-near-yemen-capital-sanaa-hit-by-saudi-arabia-houthis-say" /><summary type="html">Houthi media reported an ​attack ‌by Saudi Arabia on ⁠the ‌Yemen Oil Company ⁠in Sanaa on Tuesday, which came ‌shortly after the ‌Saudi-led coalition ​ordered the evacuation of the ​facility ​ahead of ​a military ​operation. The Iran-backed rebels had warned that they would target Riyadh's airport and other sites in the country if Saudi Arabia followed through on its threat.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>French embassy in Riyadh tells citizens to avoid Saudi airports targeted by Houthis</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e90d7d33dbfc944ebc177f087899ce6f137c29d0176886fdb55cffce4ee18d22</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-13T13:51:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261013-french-embassy-in-riyadh-tells-citizens-to-avoid-saudi-airports-targeted-by-houthis--8a1922457886.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261013-french-embassy-in-riyadh-tells-citizens-to-avoid-saudi-airports-targeted-by-houthis" /><summary type="html">France's embassy in Saudi Arabia told its citizens to avoid the airports in Riyadh, Jeddah and Dammam on Tuesday as fighting escalates between a Saudi-led coalition and Yemen's Iran-backed Houthi rebels, who have been attacking Saudi airports and oil facilities since last week.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>'Hope so heavy to carry': Al-Shaer on harrowing plight, emotional toll, struggle to survive in Gaza</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3b0ab5fbb29902b012187afe036343c02dc90e8921a88c4853092e36860555da</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-13T13:36:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/hope-so-heavy-to-carry-al-shaer-on-harrowing-plight-emotional-toll-struggle-to-survive-in-gaza--fdf8893cdd4c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/hope-so-heavy-to-carry-al-shaer-on-harrowing-plight-emotional-toll-struggle-to-survive-in-gaza" /><summary type="html">Eve Irvine is pleased to welcome Palestinian writer and poet Mahmoud Al-Shaer, “Am I real, or just a number in this war?” In I Am Still Alive: Dispatches from Gaza, Palestinian writer Mahmoud Al-Shaer explores how war transforms memory, childhood and the meaning of existence. Recounting his family's displacement, his daughter's understanding of a world reduced to tents, and his evacuation from Gaza, he reflects on writing as an act of resistance against erasure. His testimony reveals how the struggle for survival extends beyond physical destruction to the preservation of identity, collective memory and the possibility of a normal life.</summary></entry><entry><title>Airlines suspend flights to Riyadh amid security fears</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b27cbe92a8ede7519f9f88e1afbe8355b8ff3dc9c17075c78fe2dd51d3e8a4fa</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-13T09:19:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/airlines-suspend-flights-to-riyadh-amid-security-fears--7c139092e54b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/airlines-suspend-flights-to-riyadh-amid-security-fears" /><summary type="html">Flights to and from Riyadh have been suspended following deadly Houthi attacks, with several international airlines extending cancellations through October 19. The disruption threatens Saudi Arabia’s ambitions to become a major global aviation hub, while Delta has postponed its first direct US flights to the Kingdom until March 2027.</summary></entry><entry><title>No, these pictures do not show the Houthi attacks on Riyadh's King Khalid Airport</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c08be6f801575dd869a15e891c1936901073331674c7a699289bb397144d94db</id><updated>2026-10-22T13:55:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T20:59:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/truth-or-fake/20261012-no-these-pictures-do-not-show-the-houthi-attacks-on-riyadh-king-khalid-airport--5ddc7b88c7ae.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/truth-or-fake/20261012-no-these-pictures-do-not-show-the-houthi-attacks-on-riyadh-king-khalid-airport" /><summary type="html">As Saudi Arabia’s King Khalid International Airport suffers a string of deadly attacks, claimed by Yemen's Houthi rebels, a slew of AI-generated or decontextualised videos and photos have been circulating on social media, purporting to show the strikes of the last few days. We take a closer look in Truth or Fake.</summary><category term="Truth or Fake" /></entry></feed>
